From: Airtype downstream
Subject: [PATCH] mtmd : project the compute buffer one image needs

An encode grows a projector's compute buffers to the graph of the image it encodes, and the
buffers keep that size. mtmd_get_memory_usage projects them for the warmup image only, which some
projector types cap well below their largest input (set_warmup_n_tokens(256) for Pixtral and
LightOnOCR), so a caller that must fit an image into device memory before encoding it has no way
to learn how much the encode will take. For LightOnOCR-2-1B the warmup projection covers about
64 MiB while a full page grows the buffers to about 540 MiB.

This adds mtmd_get_chunk_compute_usage, which builds the graph encoding a tokenized image or audio
chunk would run and measures it with ggml_backend_sched_reserve_size, as reserve_compute_meta
measures a no_alloc projection, per backend device. It measures on a scheduler of its own and
allocates nothing, so the context's buffers stay as they are. Measuring on the context's own
scheduler would not do: when the graph outgrows the buffers, reserve_size frees them but keeps
the allocation plan, and the next encode, finding the plan current, writes through null buffers.
The declarations live in the source files because downstream patches may not change headers; the
llama-cpp-rs shim declares the function itself.

Upstream report: not yet filed; master as of 2145525a (2026-09-27) projects the warmup image only.

The projection covers the compute buffers only. A CUDA encode also takes scratch memory from the
backend's pool, which nothing projects: on a 2,896 by 2,172 page, 125 MiB beyond LightOnOCR-2-1B's
515 MiB of buffer growth and 161 MiB beyond Qwen3-VL-2B's 289 MiB.

Validated against llama.cpp b11201 on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060 Ti (CUDA 13.3), on top of
0002 and 0003: see the airtype-desktop-inference-sidecar engine's projector tests, which reserve
the projection before each encode, compare it with the device memory the encode takes, and encode
again after projecting on a context whose buffers have grown.

diff --git a/tools/mtmd/clip.cpp b/tools/mtmd/clip.cpp
index cbc2124..0f4ab8c 100644
--- a/tools/mtmd/clip.cpp
+++ b/tools/mtmd/clip.cpp
@@ -6127,6 +6127,29 @@ std::map<ggml_backend_dev_t, size_t> clip_get_mem_usage(const struct clip_ctx *
     return result;
 }
 
+// Projects the compute buffers encoding `imgs` needs on each backend device, measured the way
+// reserve_compute_meta measures a projection, without allocating them. An encode grows the buffers
+// to its graph's size, which the warmup image understates for projectors that cap it. The graph is
+// measured on a scheduler of its own: measuring on the context's scheduler would release the
+// buffers it holds while keeping their allocation plan, which the next encode would then use
+// without buffers.
+std::map<ggml_backend_dev_t, size_t> clip_get_compute_usage(struct clip_ctx * ctx, const clip_image_f32_batch * imgs);
+std::map<ggml_backend_dev_t, size_t> clip_get_compute_usage(struct clip_ctx * ctx, const clip_image_f32_batch * imgs) {
+    ggml_backend_sched_ptr sched(ggml_backend_sched_new(ctx->backend_ptrs.data(), ctx->backend_buft.data(),
+                                                        ctx->backend_ptrs.size(), ctx->max_nodes, false, true));
+    ggml_cgraph * gf = clip_get_graph_builder(ctx, *imgs)->build();
+    std::vector<size_t> sizes(ctx->backend_ptrs.size(), 0);
+    ggml_backend_sched_reserve_size(sched.get(), gf, sizes.data());
+
+    std::map<ggml_backend_dev_t, size_t> result;
+    for (size_t i = 0; i < ctx->backend_ptrs.size(); ++i) {
+        if (sizes[i] > 1) {
+            result[ggml_backend_get_device(ctx->backend_ptrs[i])] += sizes[i];
+        }
+    }
+    return result;
+}
+
